  "response": "## The hotspot map\n\nEight `high_fan_in_fan_out` findings, and they split cleanly into two kinds — **sinks** (high fan-in, things depend on them) and **hubs** (high fan-out, they depend on everything):\n\n| File | fan-in | fan-out | direct / transitive importers | also |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `src/utils.ts` | **18** | 1 | 18 / 29 | 424-line `large_file`, `jsoncParse` swallows errors |\n| `src/index.ts` | 4 | **17** | 4 / 7 | 459-line `large_file`, 5 nested god functions |\n| `src/esbuild/index.ts` | 1 | **11** | 1 / 7 | `runEsbuild` 237 lines, 38-property option bag |\n| `src/plugin.ts` | 10 | 3 | 10 / 29 | `buildFinished` 103 lines |\n| `src/log.ts` | 9 | 0 | 9 / 30 | — |\n| `test/utils.ts` | 9 | 0 | 9 / 9 | unbounded `Promise.all` |\n| `src/rollup.ts` | 0 | 7 | 0 / 0 | `getRollupConfig` 122 lines |\n| `src/api-extractor.ts` | 1 | 6 | 1 / 7 | `rollupDtsFiles` 85 lines |\n\nRepo cutoffs: fan-in p95 = 9, p99 = 10; fan-out p95 = 6, p99 = 11.\n\nOne thing the finding list hides: **`src/index.ts`'s five god functions are one nested stack, not five sites.** `build` (167–459) contains `<anonymous>` (183–456) contains `mainTasks` (260–453) contains `buildAll` (291–365) and `startWatcher` (367–445). Same for `postcss.ts` (`postcssPlugin` 6–145 wraps `setup` 18–143) and `svelte.ts` (three nested layers, 9–124). The 21 `large_function` findings are roughly 12 distinct bodies.\n\n## Triage order\n\n**0. `AGENTS.md` first (crime_00006, conf 0.90).** Not a refactor, but it's the only item here that reduces agent blast radius across all 62 files for ten minutes of work. The repo ships `bin: tsup, tsup-node` with no discoverable commands.\n\n**1. `src/utils.ts` — split by responsibility.** Highest fan-in in the repo at **18 direct importers**, 1.8× the p99 cutoff of 10, reaching **29 transitive**. It's a pure sink (fan-out 1), which is what makes it both the highest-payoff and the *lowest-risk* split: nothing it imports can cycle back, so extraction is mechanical, and a barrel re-export keeps all 18 import sites unchanged. It also hosts `jsoncParse` (crime_00049), which has `blast_radius 0.46` — the highest single value in the scan — and catches with no binding, returning `{}` so callers can't distinguish \"no config\" from \"your config is malformed.\" Fix that in the same pass.\n\nTo be clear about what \"fix\" means: the goal is not to reduce fan-in. A shared utility module being imported 18 times is doing its job. The goal is that a change to one concern inside a 424-line junk drawer stops sitting in a file 29 modules transitively load.\n\n**2. `src/index.ts` — extract the nested build stack.** Fan-out **17**, well past the p99 of 11: this is the module that knows about everything. But fan-in is only 4 direct / 7 transitive, which is the key asymmetry — **it depends on the world, the world barely depends on it.** That makes it the best payoff-to-risk ratio in the repo: you can restructure a 459-line file containing a 293-line `build` (4.9× threshold, conf 0.95) and only 4 modules can notice. Start at the innermost layer (`buildAll`, `startWatcher`) and work outward; `normalizeOptions` (96 lines) is a separate, genuinely extractable concern. Also rename or split `build`/`buildAll` (crime_00014/15) — they call `removeFiles` behind a name that reads pure.\n\n**3. `src/esbuild/index.ts` — `runEsbuild`.** Fan-out **11**, exactly at p99, and `runEsbuild` is 237 lines at 4.0× threshold with conf 0.95, reading **38 distinct properties** off one untyped `options` bag. Ranked third rather than first purely on containment: 1 direct importer, 7 transitive. The 38-property read is the real find — that's the signature of a function that should be several functions each taking a narrow record.\n\n**4. `src/plugin.ts` — `buildFinished` only.** Split judgment here: **leave the module shape alone, extract the one function.** The connectivity finding says fan-in 10, but the evidence line qualifies it — *9 of 10 importers take types only*, so that coupling is compile-time and restructuring buys nothing. `buildFinished` is different: 103 lines of runtime class method with `blast_radius 0.45` (10 direct / 29 transitive) and `test_gap 0.75`. That's the one body here where a bad edit actually propagates.\n\n## Leave alone\n\n- **`src/log.ts`** — fan-in 9 sits *at* the p95 cutoff and below p99; fan-out is 0 and 4 of 9 importers are type-only. A zero-dependency leaf logger with 30 transitive importers is a healthy shape, and it carries no size or complexity finding. There is nothing to refactor and touching it is all downside.\n- **`test/utils.ts`** — fan-in 9, fan-out 0, test harness. The unbounded `Promise.all` (crime_00020, 9 direct importers) is real but the collection is `Object.keys(files)` over author-written fixtures; N is bounded by whoever writes the test. If it ever bites, cap concurrency — don't restructure the module.\n- **`src/rollup.ts`** — despite a high-severity 122-line `getRollupConfig` and fan-out 7, every blast-radius score on this file is **0**: `blast_radius_direct_importers: 0`, transitive 0. Refactoring it relocates no risk. Worth one caveat: fan-in 0 on a live file usually means the graph missed a dynamic edge (tsup loads its rollup path in a child process), so read it as \"unmeasured,\" not \"dead.\" Don't delete anything on the strength of that zero.\n- **`src/api-extractor.ts`** — fan-out 6 is *exactly* the p95 cutoff, i.e. the weakest possible qualifying signal, with fan-in 1. Marginal inclusion; skip it.\n- **`postcss.ts` / `svelte.ts`** — genuinely high-severity god functions (140/126 and 116/106/91 lines), but 1 direct importer and 7 transitive each, and each is really one nested body. Second wave, after the four above.\n\n## Caveat on the evidence\n\nEvery finding in this scan has `churn: 0` and `recency: 0` — there's no git history signal in this fixture, so the ranking above is structural only. On a live repo I'd re-run and let churn reorder tiers 2–4; a 237-line function nobody has touched in two years is a different proposition from one edited last week. The connectivity detector also states its own limit plainly — *\"connectivity alone is not a defect\"* — which is why `log.ts` and the type-only half of `plugin.ts` come off the list despite scoring in the top tier.",
